Tantrasāra — Chapter 9
नवममाह्निकम् — Tattvabhedaprakāśanam

The Divisions of Reality: or, why seven different blokes can look at the exact same universe and somehow experience seven completely different bloody realities.

An original, irreverent retelling of Abhinavagupta’s ninth chapter, following the Sanskrit in order. The dialogue and jokes are added. Technical distinctions and the concluding verse are checked against the source text. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism +1

Right then.

Last chapter we discussed the thirty-six tattvas.

Absolutely enormous business.

We started with Śiva, watched him freely manifest his five pure powers, followed consciousness into Māyā, picked up a few limitations, acquired an ego, constructed a mind, developed five senses, and finally ended up as a bloke staring at a pot.

Magnificent.

But there’s a problem.

We’ve explained the principles of reality.

We haven’t properly explained how different beings experience those principles.

Because here’s something rather interesting.

You look at an object.

Let’s say a lovely blue flower.

You experience it in one way.

Someone else looks at the same flower and experiences it differently.

A yogin might contemplate the flower as the manifestation of a divine power.

And someone established in the supreme recognition apprehends the entire occurrence as inseparable from Śiva.

Same flower.

Very different situation.

Now, don’t misunderstand me.

I’m not merely saying people have different opinions.

“I’m fond of blue flowers.”

“Well, I prefer red ones.”

That’s not the issue.

We’re talking about different modes of conscious subjectivity.

Different forms in which the very same reality can be manifest to a knower.

And these differences are so fundamental that the Trika scriptures distinguish seven principal kinds of knowing subject.

Seven.

And each possesses its own characteristic Śakti.

And from this we’re going to derive an absolutely magnificent number.

Fifteen.

Then thirteen.

Eleven.

Nine.

Seven.

Five.

Three.

And finally, no division whatsoever.

And yes, it all means something.

Let’s begin.

1. The seven kinds of knower
Abhinavagupta opens with a simple statement.

अथ तत्त्वानां भेदो निरूप्यते।

Atha tattvānāṃ bhedo nirūpyate.

Now the divisions of the tattvas are explained.

And he says this sevenfold classification has been taught by the supreme Lord in the Ṣaḍardhaśāstra, the scriptural tradition of Trika.

Now, who are these seven?

THE SEVEN PRAMĀTṚS — KNOWING SUBJECTS

1

Śiva

Unrestricted divine subjectivity

2

Mantramaheśvara

Great lord of mantras

3

Mantreśvara

Lord of mantras

4

Mantra

