Now, before you start imagining seven completely independent species of consciousness, understand something.

These are different modes of pramātṛtva.

Knowership.

The condition of being a subject of experience.

At one extreme, we’ve got the ordinary individual.

Sakala.

You.

Me.

Everyone sitting around thinking:

“I’m this body.”

“I know certain things.”

“I don’t know other things.”

“I’d rather like a cup of tea.”

Very restricted.

At the other extreme?

Śiva.

Unrestricted consciousness.

Now the interesting question is:

What actually distinguishes these seven subjects?

Is it that they’ve got different consciousnesses?

No.

The difference arises through their characteristic powers.

Śakti.

And this is the absolutely crucial point.

Abhinavagupta says that the division between the knowers arises because of the difference between their Śaktis.

Śaktibhedād eva pramātṝṇāṃ bhedaḥ.

The difference between knowing subjects arises precisely from the differentiation of their powers.

Not because there are seven ultimate realities.

Not because seven separate consciousnesses are having a competition to see who’s the most enlightened.

But because consciousness appears through seven different modes of power.

Now let’s work out what that means.

2. Seven subjects, seven Śaktis, and one more thing
Right.

We’ve got seven possessors of power.

Śaktimantaḥ.

Each possesses a corresponding Śakti.

So that’s another seven.

Seven subjects.

Seven powers.

Fourteen.

But wait.

There’s something else.

The object itself.

Its own nature.

Svarūpa.

So:

THE FIFTEENFOLD STRUCTURE

7
Knowers

7
Powers

1
Own nature

Total aspects

15
Now, Abhinavagupta says that each tattva from earth up to Prakṛti can be distinguished in these fifteen aspects.

Every one.

Think about the earth principle.

Pṛthivī.

You can consider its nature as an object.

You can consider how it is apprehended through the power of a limited individual.

Or through the power of higher divine subjects.

And so on.

But we’re not talking about fifteen separate piles of earth.

That would be a rather disappointing metaphysical revelation.

It’s one principle understood through fifteen different aspects of its manifestation.

Now, here’s where Trika becomes particularly elegant.

Because these fifteen aspects belong to three larger divisions.

And those three correspond to three goddesses.

Yes.

The goddesses are back.

Naturally.

3. The three goddesses are running the entire cognitive operation
Now, remember Trika.

The triad.

At the heart of the tradition are three divine powers:

Parā.

Parāparā.

Aparā.

And here they take on a very precise epistemological meaning.

Not merely three beautiful goddesses adorning a diagram.

They correspond to fundamental dimensions of experience.

परा
Parā

The knowing subject — Pramātṛ

The power through which the reality of the knower is manifested.

परापरा
Parāparā

The power of knowing — Pramāṇa

The intermediary power through which the object becomes established in conscious apprehension.

अपरा
Aparā

The knowable object — Prameya

The object’s own manifest nature, capable of becoming something experienced.

And here’s the magnificent thing.

These aren’t three disconnected operations.

The object is manifested through the grace of Aparā.

Its repose in the knowing powers occurs through Parāparā.

And its repose in the knowing subject occurs through Parā.

Now, consider an ordinary experience.

You’re looking at a blue flower.

There’s the flower.

There’s the power through which it becomes known.

And there’s you, the knower.

Three aspects.

But all three belong to one conscious reality.

And the difference between the seven knowing subjects concerns how those powers are manifest.

Now, that raises a fascinating question.

If these seven subjects aren’t ultimately seven separate consciousnesses, what actually happens as we move from the ordinary individual all the way up to Śiva?

Well.

Let’s climb the ladder.

And this is where things become rather wonderful.

4. The seven powers — from ordinary bloke to unrestricted Śiva
Right.

We’re going to begin at the bottom.

Because Abhinavagupta explicitly says:

Sakalādikrameṇa.

Beginning with Sakala.

And then we’ll move upward.

First: Sakala
The ordinary limited knower.

This subject operates through two essential powers:

Vidyā and Kalā.

Limited knowledge and limited agency.

Remember those from Chapter Eight?

Vidyā means you know particular things.

Kalā means you can do particular things.

And the powers of perception and action are special expressions of these limitations.

So when you look at a flower and say:

“I see a flower.”

There’s limited cognition.

And when you reach out and pick it up:

“I’m taking this flower.”

There’s limited action.

The poor Sakala is doing his best.

He’s got a body, a mind, several sensory faculties, and a rather impressive collection of things he doesn’t know.

But he’s still operating through Śakti.

Even his limited powers are expressions of the divine.

Right.

Now let’s take away the ordinary objects.

Second: Pralayākala
Now the individual is in a condition associated with dissolution.

Pralaya.

The powers of limited knowledge and action haven’t simply become unrestricted.

But because ordinary objects are absent, these powers are no longer clearly expressed.

Asphuṭe.

Indistinct.

Imagine an entire workplace has shut down for the night.

The employees still possess their particular abilities.

The accountant can still do accounts.

The carpenter can still build furniture.

But there’s no work presently being performed.

Something of that sort.

The capacities aren’t manifesting as ordinary object-directed activity.

The powers are present in an indistinct condition.

Now, here’s the subtle point.

Absence of ordinary experience does not automatically equal liberation.

You can be free from presently appearing objects and still lack recognition of your supreme nature.

Very important.

Because otherwise we’d have to declare everyone who falls into dreamless sleep a fully liberated master.

Which would be rather generous.

Third: Vijñānākala
Now we’re moving higher.

Here the limiting powers are almost disappearing.

Vigalat-kalpe.

As though melting away.

The contracted subject no longer operates through the ordinary differentiated experience of objects.

But that doesn’t mean unrestricted divine freedom has necessarily been recognised in full.

It’s a subtler condition.

Less overt limitation.

Not yet complete fullness.

And this brings us to the divine mantra subjects.

Fourth: Mantra
Now something different happens.

Śuddhavidyā begins to awaken.

Pure knowledge.

Prabuddhyamānā.

But here’s the catch.

It still carries traces of the previous limiting powers.

Saṃskāra.

Residual impressions.

The old structures of restricted knowing haven’t entirely ceased to leave their mark.

Imagine someone’s spent their entire life wearing extremely restrictive shoes.

Then they take them off.

Wonderful.

But for a while they might still walk as though they’re wearing them.

The restriction is disappearing.

The habit remains.

That’s roughly the distinction.

Pure knowledge is awakening, but traces remain.

Fifth: Mantreśvara
Now the same Śuddhavidyā is fully awakened.

Prabuddhā.

And the residual impressions of restricted vidyā and kalā no longer accompany it.

Tatsaṃskārahīnā.

Free from those traces.

The divine knowing power is no longer coloured by the same residual limitations.

Now, this is a genuine distinction between Mantra and Mantreśvara.

Not merely that one has a slightly grander title.

The character of the knowing power itself differs.

Sixth: Mantramaheśvara
Now we’ve got an extraordinary transition.

The awakened power of pure knowledge turns toward:

Icchā-śakti.

The power of will.

And specifically, toward the character of absolute freedom.

Svātantrya.

It is moving into an increasingly explicit expression of sovereign divine will.

Now, don’t imagine that Śiva has to deliberate over what he wants.

We’re not talking about ordinary psychological desire.

This is will as a divine power.

The intrinsic freedom through which manifestation occurs.

And finally:

Seventh: Śiva
Here, will is explicitly of the nature of absolute freedom.

Sphuṭa-svātantrya.

Unrestricted.

Not merely the ability to select between a few available possibilities.

Not:

“I’ll have tea instead of coffee.”

But the freedom belonging to consciousness itself.

And there we have the seven powers.

Now here’s the critical point.

Different powers produce different modes of knowership.

The differences between subjects arise because of the differences in the instruments through which they operate.

But what happens if we go deeper?

What is the actual conscious reality underlying all seven?

Abhinavagupta gives us one of the most important statements in this chapter.

5. There’s actually only one knower
Here it is:

Vastutaḥ punar eka eva.

In reality, however, there is only one.

One what?

Pramātā.

Knower.

And what’s this knower’s essential condition?

Cit-svātantryānanda-viśrāntaḥ.

Resting in the bliss of the freedom of consciousness.

Now read that again.

In reality, there is only one knower, resting in the bliss of conscious freedom.

The seven kinds of subject aren’t seven competing ultimate selves.

They’re seven manifestations of subjectivity according to the predominance of different Śaktis.

And this raises an important philosophical question.

How can different instruments of knowing produce different kinds of knower?

Well, suppose you try to make the instruments entirely independent of consciousness.

Now who’s going to connect them to the knower?

Another instrument?

And who’s going to connect that one?

Another?

And another?

Bloody hell.

Infinite regress again.

Anavasthā.

We’ve already had enough of this nonsense.

So the knowing instrument must be inseparable from the power of consciousness.

Śakti herself can function as the instrument.

Because Śakti isn’t something foreign to Śiva.

She is his own power.

And therefore, when the knowing power differs, the mode of knowing subjectivity differs too.

But the ultimate knower remains one.

Now, let’s make this concrete.

Suppose someone called Caitra sees a blue object.

And you know what Caitra has seen.

Now you can say:

“I know what Caitra saw.”

Or:

“I know what Caitra knew.”

And this introduces something beyond the object’s bare nature.

You’re now apprehending the object in relation to another subject’s cognition.

So the same object can appear through different modes of knowing.

The object itself.

The object as known by an ordinary individual.

The object as apprehended through the higher powers of consciousness.

All the way up to its repose in the nature of Śiva.

And this is why we can distinguish fifteen aspects without requiring fifteen independent objects.

But now someone’s going to interrupt.

There’s always one.

“Abhinava, excuse me.”

Go on.

“If everything’s consciousness, and the same object can be experienced differently, how do you know the object actually exists?”

Ah.

Excellent question.

Because we’re about to deal with the reality of the shared world.

6. Is the blue object real, or are we all just imagining it?
Right.

Suppose you’ve got a blue object.

Very blue.

Absolutely magnificent shade of blue.

And someone says:

“Well, if its nature is to be known, shouldn’t absolutely everyone know it?”

Interesting.

Because if knowability were simply an inherent property automatically forcing cognition upon everyone, then everything would be known by everyone.

And that obviously isn’t how ordinary experience works.

Now, perhaps you’ll say:

“Well, things have the property of being knowable.”

But then is that property itself knowable?

And does it have another property of knowability?

And another?

Congratulations.

You’ve just constructed another infinite regress.

Now, there is a second problem.

If the object is simultaneously intrinsically knowable and unknowable, you appear to be attributing incompatible properties to the same thing.

Viruddha-dharma-yoga.

Contradictory characteristics.

And Abhinavagupta doesn’t accept that.

So how does he resolve the issue?

By distinguishing the object’s own nature from the form in which it rests within a knowing power or subject.

The object’s essential nature is not identical with every mode of its being known.

When it appears through a particular Śakti, that apprehended form shines through consciousness.

Svaprakāśa.

Self-luminous.

It doesn’t require an endless additional chain of cognitions to illuminate it.

Now there’s another interesting point.

Even if innumerable subjects experience one object, it doesn’t follow that the object must become innumerably many independent objects.

Abhinavagupta says that its form can remain one across the multiplicity of subjects.

And this is why experience of a shared world remains intelligible.

But is that world actually real?

He says yes.

And gives the criterion:

Arthakriyākāritva.

The capacity to produce an effect or perform a function.

Now, that’s interesting.

Because we’re not merely discussing private thoughts that have no consequences.

We’re dealing with appearances that operate within a shared field of experience.

And Abhinavagupta gives an example that’s rather funny.

7. The beautiful woman, the jealous bloke, and the dancing girl
Imagine you see a beautiful woman.

And other people are looking at her.

Now you become jealous.

“Who the hell is that bloke looking at?”

“Why’s he looking at her?”

“What’s going on here?”

Absolutely ridiculous.

But notice what’s happened.

Your experience has been affected by the fact that another person is apprehending the same woman.

So the object isn’t being understood merely as a private image locked inside one individual mind.

There’s a shared field in which subjects and their experiences are related.

And the object possesses efficacy within that field.

Now Abhinavagupta gives another example.

A pot.

Kumbha.

But not simply any pot.

A pot contemplated as a support for repose in the nature of Śiva.

You look at this vessel.

And through the appropriate contemplative understanding, you apprehend it as belonging to Śiva’s own nature.

What happens?

Samāviśati.

You enter into absorption.

The pot becomes a means of immersion into divine consciousness.

And then another example.

A dancing girl.

Nartakī.

You watch her performance.

The same appearance can be enjoyed by numerous spectators, and in the aesthetic experience there’s a kind of fullness.

Pūrṇībhavati.

You become filled.

Now, this isn’t Abhinavagupta saying that watching a dancing girl automatically liberates everyone.

Please don’t start booking tickets and calling it your highest sādhana.

His point is subtler.

One object can manifest through many subjects, and its different modes of apprehension can have genuinely different experiential effects.

Jealousy.

Absorption.

Aesthetic fullness.

The object isn’t necessarily different in every case.

The mode of apprehension differs.

And this is the perfect example of what we’re studying.

Pramātṛ-bheda.

The differentiation of knowing subjects.

Now, you might be wondering:

“Right, is everything fifteenfold, then?”

Not quite.

Because as we move upward through the tattvas, the number of distinctions begins to decrease.

And this is where the mathematics gets rather beautiful.

8. The divine mathematics of recognition
Right.

We’ve got fifteen aspects at the level of the lower tattvas.

One own-nature.

Seven powers.

Seven subjects.

But as we ascend through the hierarchy, something happens.

Certain lower kinds of knower are no longer capable of functioning as subjects at the higher level.

And so the relevant pair disappears.

The power.

And the possessor of that power.

Two distinctions at a time.

So we get:

