Śiva — complete fullness

No division
Now, let’s understand what this actually means.

From earth through Prakṛti, we can make the complete fifteenfold distinction.

But from Puruṣa through Kalā, there’s no room for the Sakala to operate as a knowing subject in the same manner.

So its power-and-subject pair disappears.

Fifteen becomes thirteen.

Then the Pralayākala pair ceases to function as such.

Thirteen becomes eleven.

Then the Vijñānākala pair.

Eleven becomes nine.

Then Mantra.

Nine becomes seven.

Then Mantreśvara.

Seven becomes five.

Then Mantramaheśvara.

Five becomes three.

And finally?

Śiva.

Here there is no division.

Na ko’pi bhedaḥ.

Why?

Paripūrṇatvāt.

Because of complete fullness.

Now, notice something.

We aren’t simply deleting pieces of reality.

We’re reducing the distinctions by which one reality is apprehended.

At lower levels, consciousness appears through differentiated subjects and powers.

At higher levels, those distinctions cease to be independently applicable.

And in Śiva, consciousness is completely full.

No further division.

No remaining separate subject standing over against it.

Now, this is a particularly beautiful account of nonduality.

Because multiplicity doesn’t have to be destroyed.

It’s recognised as a differentiation of one reality.

And the more comprehensive the standpoint, the fewer independent distinctions are required.

The seven knowing subjects are not seven different substances. They’re differentiations of one conscious freedom.

And this is why Abhinavagupta says that the divisions of the tattvas account for the diversity of the worlds.

Remember Chapter Seven?

The hell realms.

The heavenly realms.

The worlds of the Rudras.

All of them may belong to the earth principle.

And yet they’re radically different in character.

How?

Because their manifestations differ according to the subjects and powers through which they are established.

The same broad tattva can contain vastly different worlds of experience.

Now, if you want every possible subdivision explained in exhausting detail, Abhinavagupta has some advice.

Read the Tantrāloka.

Yes.

He’s recommending his own enormous book again.

Fair enough.

But for now, let’s make the whole thing concrete.

Because he’s going to take the earth principle and explain how all seven subjects relate to it.

9. One earth, seven ways of being its knower
Right.

Take the earth tattva.

Dharā-tattva.

And let’s examine the entire thing as both knowable and knowing.

First:

Śiva.

The consciousness that is entirely non-different from the earth principle is Śiva.

And Abhinavagupta cites a scriptural saying:

Pṛthivy evedaṃ brahma.

This earth itself is Brahman.

Now think about that.

Not merely:

“Śiva created the earth.”

But:

The reality of the earth is non-different from the supreme luminosity.

Then comes:

Dharā-mantramaheśvara.

The great lord associated with the earth principle.

He impels those divine powers who grant attainments connected with that principle.

Siddhi.

Then:

Dharā-mantreśvara.

The lord of mantras associated with earth, acting under that higher direction.

Then:

Dharā-mantra.

The mantra associated with that divine power.

Here Abhinavagupta describes it as a denoting expression, vācaka, connected with the particular divine form apprehended.

Now, don’t imagine these are merely three human jobs with different salary bands.

They’re hierarchically differentiated forms of divine subjectivity associated with the earth principle.

Then:

Dharā-vijñānākala.

This is someone who has practised the yoga of the earth principle through Śaiva knowledge, rising beyond the limited knowledge of systems such as Sāṃkhya, but who has not yet attained the stable supreme condition.

Aprāpta-dhruvapada.

The firm abode hasn’t been reached.

Then:

Dharā-pralayākala.

Someone whose practice has proceeded through a more limited understanding, and who enters a corresponding dissolution condition at death or at the end of a cosmic age.

Now here’s something worth noting.

Abhinavagupta mentions the emergence of varied dreams through absorption in the tattva, even in conditions associated with deep sleep.

The point is that withdrawal from gross waking objects doesn’t necessarily eliminate the subtle capacity for differentiated manifestations.

And finally:

Dharā-sakala.

The ordinary individual who identifies with earth.

Gṛhīta-dharābhimānaḥ.

One who has taken on identification with the earth principle.

Now there’s our good old limited individual again.

The one who identifies with the body and the physical world.

And Abhinavagupta says that even here, the fourteen subject-and-power distinctions remain possible according to the predominance and subordination of Śakti.

With the object’s own nature, we return to fifteen.

And the same procedure applies to the other tattvas.

Now, that’s important.

Because what we’ve been doing is not merely classifying different kinds of souls.

We’re showing how a single cosmic principle can be apprehended at multiple degrees of subjectivity.

But here’s where the chapter becomes particularly astonishing.

Because Abhinavagupta is going to take this enormous metaphysical structure and apply it to something incredibly ordinary.

Seeing the colour blue.

And apparently, the entire hierarchy of consciousness can be traced through sixteen little divisions of prāṇa.

You thought we were finished with breath arithmetic?

Absolutely not.

10. How seeing something blue contains the whole structure of awareness
Right.

Imagine looking at something blue.

A blue flower, perhaps.

Or the sky.

Very nice.

Now, Abhinavagupta says that the movement of vital energy associated with this apprehension can be analysed into:

Sixteen tuṭis.

Tuṭiṣoḍaśaka.

Sixteen minute divisions.

Remember our earlier treatment of prāṇa and its measures?

We’re using that same traditional subtle-body framework, not reporting experimentally measured stages of colour perception.

And the question is:

What happens between the initial undivided awareness and the fully manifest experience of the object?

Let’s examine the sequence.

The first tuṭi — before the split
The first is:

Avibhāgaikarūpā.

Of one undivided nature.

There isn’t yet the fully articulated distinction between the one who grasps and the thing grasped.

Undivided.

Now, this doesn’t mean unconsciousness.

It means that the differentiation of subject and object has not yet become explicit.

The second tuṭi — the knower begins to emerge
Now we have:

Grāhakollāsa.

The arising or blossoming of the grasper.

The knower begins to manifest as a distinct aspect of cognition.

Notice how subtle this is.

Abhinavagupta isn’t starting with a fully formed individual who then decides to look at something.

He’s analysing the very emergence of the relation between subject and object.

And now we enter the middle twelve divisions.

This is where it gets properly complicated.

The middle twelve tuṭis
The first six of these twelve are associated with:

Nirvikalpa.

Nonconceptual awareness.

And they conceal the emergence of conceptual determination.

Vikalpācchādaka.

Now, here’s an important distinction.

Nirvikalpa doesn’t simply mean that absolutely nothing is happening.

There’s an intricate process through which differentiated apprehension is emerging.

But it hasn’t yet become fully conceptual.

Abhinavagupta distinguishes the object’s essential nature and five aspects of the conceptual determination that is still concealed.

These include movements described through:

Unmimiṣā.

The inclination to open.

Unmiṣattā.

The process of opening.

Unmiṣitatā.

The condition of having opened.

And the emergence of agency in relation to its own operation.

Now, notice the verb family.

Unmiṣ.

Opening.

Unfolding.

The same beautiful imagery we encountered when discussing divine manifestation.

The world opens into appearance.

But there’s a reason this can’t be reduced to one isolated instant.

Abhinavagupta points out that a pulsation isn’t simply a single moment without internal articulation.

Spanda involves an unfolding.

So the intermediate phases possess further divisions.

Now the next six tuṭis involve the fading of the nonconceptual condition, the fading of conceptual differentiation, and further movements of opening toward explicit manifestation.

The passage is technically compressed, and its precise allocation of the subsidiary phases is difficult. But the overall structure is clear.

Cognition moves from undivided awareness through subtle articulation toward explicit differentiation.

Then we approach the final two divisions.

The penultimate tuṭi — the knower becomes explicit
Here:

Sphuṭībhūta-grāhaka.

The knower has become clearly manifest.

The subject is now explicit within the structure of the experience.

And finally:

The sixteenth tuṭi — the object is fully present
The final division is:

Grāhyābhinna.

Non-different from the grasped object.

Now the apprehension has reached the object in its manifest form.

You’re seeing blue.

And you think:

“Well, I’ve just seen blue. What’s all the fuss?”

But Abhinavagupta is analysing an entire structure of manifestation concealed within that apparently simple experience.

Undivided awareness.

Emergence of the subject.

Subtle nonconceptual articulation.

Conceptual determination.

Explicit knower.

Manifest object.

And here’s the crucial point.

Even the simplest ordinary perception is not metaphysically simple.

It’s an articulation of consciousness through the relation between knower, knowing power and known object.

And he says the discerning practitioners who study their teacher’s instructions can distinguish the fifteenfold structure throughout experience.

Now, here’s where it gets especially interesting.

Because when conceptual differentiation diminishes, the corresponding divisions of this process diminish too.

And that brings us to something very close to liberation.

11. As concepts disappear, something extraordinary happens
Now, here’s the fascinating part.

Abhinavagupta says:

Vikalpanyūnatve tu tuṭinyūnatā.

As conceptual differentiation decreases, the number of tuṭis decreases.

Think about that.

The more elaborate your conceptual apprehension, the more differentiated its manifestation.

But as those conceptual distinctions fall away, the process becomes simpler.

More immediate.

And he gives the example of happiness.

Sukha.

Sometimes, when you’re completely absorbed in delight, there’s very little explicit conceptual division.

You’re not standing there thinking:

“Ah yes, I am the experiencer, this is the experience, this is my cognitive instrument, and I’m currently experiencing pleasure.”

You’re simply absorbed.

Now, don’t confuse ordinary happiness with full liberation.

But it helps illustrate how explicit conceptual distinctions can recede.

And here’s where Abhinavagupta makes a very subtle observation.

Most ordinary people don’t clearly recognise the nonconceptual ground of awareness.

Vimarśabhūmi.

The field of self-awareness.

Why?

Because conceptual activity occupies their attention.

It’s as though the nonconceptual ground has been hidden by the very distinctions appearing within it.

Now, consider what happens when those distinctions decrease.

The ground doesn’t have to be manufactured.

It becomes manifest.

Prakāśate.

It’s revealed as conceptual determination subsides.

And Abhinavagupta identifies this with careful attentiveness to the relation between the grasper and the grasped.

Grāhya-grāhaka-sambandhe sāvadhānatā.

Now that’s an absolutely magnificent instruction.

Because he’s not merely saying:

“Stop thinking.”

He’s asking you to investigate the relation between subject and object with extreme precision.

What is actually occurring when something is experienced?

How does the apparent object emerge?

How does the knowing subject emerge?

And what is the awareness through which their relation is manifest?

Now, as the differentiation of explicit idantā — this-ness — diminishes, the corresponding divisions disappear in pairs.

Two at a time.

Sixteen.

Fourteen.

Twelve.

Ten.

Eight.

Six.

Four.

And finally:

Two.

Dvituṭikaḥ śivāveśaḥ.

Immersion in Śiva characterised by two tuṭis.

Now, don’t turn this into a challenge to suppress your breathing to an impossibly small movement.

This is an esoteric account of the reduction of differentiated manifestation, expressed through traditional measures of prāṇa.

And Abhinavagupta says that at this final stage, the two divisions have distinct characteristics.

The first is:

Sarvataḥ pūrṇā.

Full in every respect.

Completely replete.

Nothing lacking.

But the second is associated with the manifestation of omniscience and omnipotence through cultivation.

Sarvajñatva.

All-knowingness.

Sarvakartṛtva.

All-agency.

And why isn’t the first division described as producing those attainments?

Because the first is already full.

There’s nothing to acquire.

The second permits an expression of powers through differentiation.

Now that is remarkably elegant.

At the most fundamental level, fullness doesn’t require an accomplishment.

But when the divine power manifests through a differentiated mode, particular attainments can arise.

Fullness itself is not an achievement. Manifest powers belong to its expressive freedom.

And then Abhinavagupta invokes an earlier teacher.

One you really ought to know.

Kallaṭa.

The great early Spanda master.

And a particular expression:

Tuṭipāta.

The falling or descent of a tuṭi.

But then Abhinavagupta does something absolutely outrageous.

He connects the meaning of pāta with:

Śrīmatī Kālī.

The revered goddess Kālī.

And:

Mātṛsadbhāva.

The true being of the knowing subject.

And:

Bhairava.

And:

Pratibhā.

Immediate illuminating insight.

Now, imagine that.

We started by looking at a blue object.

We’ve passed through sixteen minute divisions of cognition.

Then reduced them through diminishing conceptual differentiation.

And now the very principle of that transition is being identified with Kālī and Bhairava.

The goddess of cosmic manifestation and withdrawal is also implicated in the most intimate transition within awareness.

Absolutely beautiful.

And then Abhinavagupta says:

Alam rahasyārahasyanena.

Enough of disclosing the secret.

Oh, come on, mate.

You’ve just introduced the supreme goddess into a discussion of minute divisions of perception and now you’re calling it a day?

Apparently so.

He does say that practising the corresponding stages, beginning with that of Mantramaheśvara, is traditionally held to produce their respective attainments.

But he doesn’t fully unpack the esoteric teaching here.

So we won’t pretend he has.

Right.

Now, after all that, we come to something exceptionally important.

The states of consciousness.

Waking.

Dreaming.

Deep sleep.

The fourth.

And beyond the fourth.

And here’s where the chapter becomes philosophically exquisite.

12. Waking, dreaming and deep sleep aren’t simply things that happen to you
Right.

Ordinarily, you think of the states like this.

You’re awake.

Then you go to sleep.

Perhaps you dream.

Perhaps you don’t.

Then you wake up.

Very straightforward.

But Abhinavagupta says a state of consciousness is not simply a property of the object.

Nor simply a property of consciousness considered in isolation.

Nor two independent things standing side by side.

It’s the particular relation between the knowable and the awareness apprehending it.

Anyonyāpekṣa.

Mutual dependence.

Now, that’s a very important idea.

Because we’re not talking about a consciousness existing completely apart from all its manifestations, with states merely arriving from somewhere else.

We’re discussing the configurations through which experience becomes manifest.

Let’s begin with waking.

Jāgrat — waking
When the object appears as external, as something standing before the knower, we call that:

Jāgrat.

Waking.

The object appears outside.

The knower apprehends it.

The knowing process relates them.

Now consider the statement:

“There’s a flower over there.”

That’s a familiar waking configuration.

The object is apprehended in an externalised form.

Bahirūpatā.

But now suppose the form is no longer appearing as an externally established object.

Instead, it appears through ideation.

Svapna — dreaming
Saṅkalpa.

Mental construction.

Now the appearance is constituted differently.

Think about dreaming of a flower.

You can see it.

You can smell it.

You can even imagine picking it up.

But the appearance doesn’t possess the same relation to waking externality.

The object appears within a different configuration of awareness.

And that’s dreaming.

But now go further.

Suppose the differentiated appearance doesn’t manifest explicitly at all.

Instead, it remains in the condition of a seed.

Suṣupti — deep sleep
Bījātmatā.

The condition of being present in seed form.

The manifest object has subsided.

The differentiated appearance is not present in the ordinary way.

But the possibility of manifestation remains.

Now here’s something crucial.

Abhinavagupta isn’t simply equating deep sleep with the supreme condition of liberation.

The absence of differentiated experience is still a particular condition.

And it’s connected with the subject in whom those appearances may arise.

Now, he says each of these states can be considered in relation to the knowable, the means of knowledge and the knower.

And each has further subdivisions.

But there is something beyond the three.

Because we’ve still not arrived at the fundamental freedom of consciousness.

And that brings us to:

13. Turya — the fourth state
Turya.

Literally:

The fourth.

Now, in the familiar analysis, waking, dreaming and deep sleep are three modes of experience.

But Turya is not merely one more mode alongside them.

It’s the apprehension of the fullness of the knower.

Pramātuḥ pūrṇatā.

And Abhinavagupta gives a beautiful progression.

Ordinarily, you have three aspects.

The form.

Rūpam.

The seeing faculty.

Dṛśā.

And:

Ah am.

The I.

Or, properly joined:

Aham.

Now imagine the sentence:

“I see a form.”

You’ve got an object.

An instrument.

And a subject.

But in Turya, the separate conceptualisation of these aspects is transcended in the immediate apprehension:

Paśyāmi.

I see.

And here, the act of knowing isn’t grounded in a separately constructed instrument.

It’s an expression of immediate subjectivity.

Anupāyikā pramātṛtā.

Knowership without an intervening means.

And its essence is:

Svātantrya.

Freedom.

Now, don’t misunderstand this.

Abhinavagupta isn’t saying every ordinary use of the phrase “I see” is the fourth state.

That would be a remarkably easy path to liberation.

You could walk around announcing:

“I see!”

And be fully enlightened by lunchtime.

No.

He’s pointing toward the immediate conscious agency underlying the differentiated relation between seeing, seer and seen.

And this Turya has three aspects corresponding to its relation to the previous three states.

It bestows grace upon them.

Anugraha.

It illumines their underlying nature.

So the fourth doesn’t merely annihilate waking, dreaming and deep sleep.

It reveals the freedom through which they appear.

Now, there are different traditional names for these states.

And Abhinavagupta gives two sets.

The yogins call them:

Piṇḍastha.

Padastha.

Rūpastha.

Rūpātīta.

Those devoted to systematic contemplative discernment use:

Sarvatobhadra.

Vyāpti.

Mahāvyāpti.

Pracaya.

And he refers to the Tantrāloka and his metrical commentary for the detailed explanations.

So he’s explicitly giving us the terminology while reserving the fuller analysis for other works.

But there’s still one more state.

And this one’s particularly important.

14. Turyātīta — beyond even the fourth
Now we’re talking.

Turyātīta.

T urya — the fourth.

Atīta — gone beyond, transcended.

Properly joined:

Turyātīta.

Beyond the fourth.

And what is this?

The fullness that contains everything while transcending everything.

Sarvāntarbhūta.

Containing all.

Sarvātīta.

Beyond all.

Now, that should sound familiar.

Remember how we began the whole Tantrasāra?

Śiva is both immanent and transcendent.

Viśvamaya.

Of the nature of the universe.

Viśvottīrṇa.

Beyond the universe.

Well, here’s the same fundamental principle expressed through the analysis of states.

The supreme reality is not merely one state competing with other states.

It is the fullness in which all states arise.

And Abhinavagupta gives another name.

Mahāpracaya.

The great fullness or supreme accumulation.

Now, this is important.

Because if you imagine liberation as entering Turya and staying there forever, you’ve still potentially constructed an opposition between the fourth state and ordinary waking experience.

But Turyātīta is not defined through that exclusion.

It’s the all-inclusive fullness that transcends every limitation.

Not merely consciousness without the world, but consciousness whose freedom includes the entire world.

And this brings us back to the seven subjects.

Because Abhinavagupta is about to connect the five states of consciousness with the various levels of knowership.

15. Different subjects inhabit different kinds of experience
Now let’s examine the states again.

Waking.

Dreaming.

Deep sleep.

Turya.

Turyātīta.

And here’s an important qualification.

Abhinavagupta says the term waking can be used wherever an appearance is clear, stable and continuously connected.

Sphuṭa.

Clear.

Sthira.

Stable.

Anubandhin.

Connected or enduring.

Dreaming is the contrary condition, where that stable clarity isn’t present.

And then he associates the states with different levels of the knowing subject.

The experience of a Pralayākala corresponds to the dreaming condition.

That of a Vijñānākala to a condition of nonapprehension associated with deep sleep.

That of the Mantra class and the higher mantra lords to Turya, in which the knowable is brought into unity.

And finally:

Śiva.

The condition of non-difference between every existent thing and Śiva.

Bhāvānāṃ śivābhedaḥ.

That’s Turyātīta.

Now isn’t that interesting?

The same categories we’ve been analysing as kinds of subjectivity can also be understood through the configurations of conscious experience.

And Abhinavagupta then gives a series of correspondences between the different numerical divisions and the five states.

In the fifteenfold system, the own-nature of the object together with Sakala is associated with waking.

Pralayākala with dreaming.

Vijñānākala with deep sleep.

The group comprising Mantra, Mantreśvara and Mantramaheśvara with Turya.

And Śiva with Turyātīta.

But as we move upward through the thirteenfold, elevenfold, ninefold, sevenfold, fivefold and threefold arrangements, the specific allocations change.

Because the lower forms of knowing subjectivity have ceased to apply.

And even within the undivided Śiva-tattva, contemplative analysis can provisionally distinguish five aspects through:

Kriyā.

Action.

Jñāna.

Knowledge.

Icchā.

Will.

Ānanda.

Bliss.

Cit.

Consciousness.

Now notice the beautiful circularity.

We began Chapter Eight with Śiva’s five powers.

Then followed those powers downward into increasingly differentiated manifestation.

Now, at the end of Chapter Nine, we’ve arrived back at those same five powers.

But we’ve understood that even their distinction is an analytical construction within the fullness of Śiva.

The same consciousness manifests the many, experiences the many, and recognises the many as itself.

And now Abhinavagupta gives his actual concluding verse.

Yes.

There really is one.

And it is absolutely magnificent.

16. The concluding verse — nothing in the universe is outside the knowing Self
ORIGINAL SANSKRIT — CHAPTER NINE

भूम्यादौ तत्त्वजाले न हि भवति वपुस्तादृशं यत्प्रमातुः।

संविद्विश्रान्तिवन्ध्यं स्फुरति स बहुधा मातृभावोऽस्य यस्मात्॥

