Bahir-upāsya-mantra-tādātmya.

Now, tādātmya is a magnificent word.

Tad — that.

Ātmya — having the nature of, identity with.

Being of the same nature as that.

So the practitioner doesn’t merely think:

“Here’s an external substance, and I’m going to perform some magic upon it.”

Instead, the ritual substance is made to possess the character of the mantra.

Tanmayīkṛta.

Made of that.

Permeated by that.

And then:

Nimajjana.

Immersion.

Now, here’s the extraordinary thing.

You aren’t simply placing your body in some ordinary material.

Within the contemplative ritual, you’re immersing yourself in a form recognised as divine.

The object and mantra are brought into identity.

The mantra expresses a power of Śiva.

And the practitioner enters into that manifestation.

Now imagine the difference between two people taking a bath.

The first thinks:

“Right. Water. Soap. Clean body.”

Very sensible.

The second undertakes the ritual contemplation:

“This water is a manifestation of Parameśvara. The mantra is the expression of divine power. My body is itself a manifestation of that same consciousness.”

Now, we shouldn’t pretend that simply thinking those words is equivalent to completing the formal practice.

But the conceptual distinction is clear.

The external rite is designed to transform how the practitioner apprehends the substance, the body and the deity.

And what happens when their separation is overcome?

Samāveśa.

Divine immersion.

Now, that’s the basic external form.

But Abhinavagupta then introduces something much more specialised.

And it’s one of the most esoteric passages in this short chapter.

7. The vessel of bliss — and the entire deity-wheel inside it
Right.

Now we’re going to discuss:

Ānandadravya.

The substance of bliss.

Within this vīra ritual, the substance is wine.

And it’s held in something called:

Vīrādhāra.

The vessel or receptacle of the hero.

Now, before anyone starts pouring themselves a glass and announcing they’ve become Bhairava, understand that we’re describing a specific traditional ritual, not ordinary alcohol consumption.

And Abhinavagupta gives a sequence.

First, the practitioner looks upon the substance.

Nirīkṣaṇa.

Seeing.

But not simply observing its ordinary physical characteristics.

The substance is made to possess the nature of Śiva.

Śivamayīkṛtya.

Having made it pervaded by Śiva.

Now, look at that verb.

Śivamaya — consisting of Śiva, pervaded by Śiva.

Kṛtya — having made.

The ritual is transforming the practitioner’s apprehension of the substance.

And here’s what happens next.

Mantracakrapūjana.

Worship of the wheel of mantras.

Now, cakra here isn’t simply one of those coloured diagrams of energy centres you see on yoga posters.

It refers to an organised group or circle of divine mantra powers.

And those mantras are worshipped within the consecrated vessel.

So what have we got?

The substance.

The vessel.

The mantra-wheel.

And the divine powers expressed through it.

All drawn into one ritual field.

But now comes the extraordinary part.

Abhinavagupta says the consecrated substance is then employed for:

Deha-prāṇobhayāśrita-devatācakra-tarpaṇa.

Bloody hell.

Another compound.

Let’s unpack this beautiful monster.

Deha — body.

Prāṇa — vital energy.

Ubhaya — both.

Āśrita — residing in, supported by.

Devatā-cakra — circle of deities.

Tarpaṇa — satisfying, nourishing or ritually offering libations.

So:

The ritual satisfying of the circle of deities residing in both the body and vital energy.

Now, that’s absolutely magnificent.

Because the sacred substance isn’t merely being offered to some deity sitting outside the practitioner.

The body and prāṇa themselves are understood as containing or supporting divine powers.

Remember our earlier chapters?

Prāṇa.

Apāna.

Samāna.

Udāna.

Vyāna.

All the subtle processes of embodied manifestation.

And remember the idea that the body can become a support for the entire cosmic path?

Well, here’s another way in which the body becomes a sacred field.

The deities associated with body and prāṇa are ritually nourished through the consecrated substance.

And Abhinavagupta calls this:

Mukhyaṃ snānam.

The principal bath.

Now that’s astonishing.

Because someone might have expected an elaborate instruction about pouring water over the head.

Instead, we’re discussing a mantra-wheel installed in a consecrated vessel and the ritual satisfaction of divine powers embodied within the practitioner.

And here’s the philosophical point.

The body is not merely an impure obstacle to divine consciousness. It can be recognised as a field of divine powers.

But the ritual requires more than merely possessing the right substances.

What makes it sacred is the process by which those substances, mantras, bodily powers and the worshipper are apprehended through their relationship to Śiva.

That’s the underlying structure.

And now, at last, Abhinavagupta returns to the internal bath.

And this is where the chapter reaches its natural culmination.

8. Internal bathing — you don’t actually need to get wet
Right.

Ābhyantara-snāna.

Internal bathing.

And the Sanskrit is remarkably concise.

Abhinavagupta says that through the contemplative assumption of the forms of earth and the other elements, the practitioner becomes identified with the corresponding circles or groups.

Pārthivādi-cakra.

Now the key word is:

Tanmayībhāva.

The condition of becoming of that nature.

And the method is:

Dhāraṇā.

Sustained contemplation.

Now, what does that mean?

Let’s begin with earth.

You contemplate the form of earth.

Not merely as a lump of soil.

But as a manifestation of divine power.

And through sustained contemplation, you become identified with the relevant divine form.

Then water.

Then the remaining forms, according to their appropriate contemplative arrangement.

But here’s the crucial distinction.

The process is taking place through consciousness itself.

You’re not literally transforming the chemical composition of your body into earth, water, fire or sky.

You’re cultivating identification with the divine reality expressed through those forms.

And now consider something extraordinary.

We began the chapter by defining impurity as the conviction that the Self is mixed with something alien to its nature.

And now the internal bath consists of overcoming that separation through contemplative identification.

So the whole chapter is remarkably coherent.

The problem:

Abhimāna.

Mistaken identification.

The remedy:

Samāveśa.

Immersion in the supreme nature.

And the ritual method:

Tanmayībhāva.

Becoming identified with the divine form.

Now, imagine someone asking:

“Abhinava, mate, why bother with all these different forms? Why earth, water, wind and the rest?”

Because different practitioners require different supports.

Remember what we established at the beginning?

Some people recognise directly.

Others require one or several methods.

And these methods may operate through the body, external substances, mantras or contemplation.

But the important thing is what they accomplish.

They overcome the sense that consciousness is fundamentally separate from the manifestations through which it appears.

Now, here’s the extraordinary implication.

You don’t purify the Self by turning it into Śiva.

It already possesses the nature of Śiva.

You purify the mistaken identification that obscures that recognition.

And whether the means involves water, fire, mantra, a consecrated substance or contemplative absorption, the decisive point remains:

Parameśvarasvarūpasamāveśa.

Immersion in the essential nature of the Supreme Lord.

Now that’s a proper bath.

And it brings us to the actual closing verses.

9. The concluding Sanskrit verse — the real bath is immersion in Bhairava
ATTESTED SANSKRIT — CHAPTER TWELVE

