Right.

Here it is.

The entire teaching.

Parabhairavasammajjanam.

Immersion in supreme Bhairava.

Para — supreme.

Bhairava — the supreme Lord.

Sam-majjana — immersion, plunging into.

And this is what is called bathing.

Snāna.

Now, what about external bathing?

It’s secondary.

Na mukhyam.

Not the principal thing.

Upacārataḥ.

Figuratively or by conventional ritual designation.

And that’s a magnificent distinction.

Because Abhinavagupta isn’t saying you shouldn’t wash your body.

He’s saying that washing the body and attaining supreme purity are not identical accomplishments.

You can wash away dirt.

You can put on clean clothes.

You can smell absolutely delightful.

But if you’re still completely convinced that your consciousness is fundamentally separate from the universe and the supreme Lord, the metaphysical impurity he’s describing hasn’t been overcome.

Conversely, the highest purification is not dependent upon the physical cleanliness of the body.

It’s the recognition of one’s true nature.

Now, one additional textual detail.

A Sanskrit textual witness also preserves a further closing stanza in a Middle Indo-Aryan vernacular form, beginning:

