And now let’s finish in our own words.

You’ve spent your entire life imagining that purity is something you can acquire.

Something you can achieve.

Something you can produce through the right action.

And perhaps you’ve believed that impurity is something genuinely stuck to your essential being.

Some terrible contamination.

Some condition that separates you from the divine.

But Abhinavagupta says:

Hang on, mate.

What exactly could contaminate unrestricted consciousness?

Nothing stands independently outside it.

Even the apparent impurities are manifested through its own freedom.

The problem isn’t that Śiva has somehow become fundamentally impure.

The problem is that consciousness has taken on an identification that conceals its own nature.

And the moment that identification is overcome?

Purity.

Not because something foreign has been removed from the supreme Self.

But because its nature has become evident.

So you can enter a river.

You can bathe in sacred water.

You can contemplate earth.

Fire.

Wind.

Space.

The Moon.

The Sun.

You can perform mantra-nyāsa.

You can engage in internal contemplation.

And all these may serve as supports.

But the highest bath?

Immersion in Parabhairava.

The supreme consciousness that is never separate from your own essential being.

And that’s the punchline.

You thought you needed to wash something off yourself to reach Śiva.

Abhinavagupta says the very conviction that you’re something other than Śiva is the impurity.

You don’t wash yourself until you become Śiva. You recognise that even the apparent impurity never possessed an independent reality outside Śiva.

And that recognition is the real purification.

Absolutely bloody magnificent.

